          "nodes": [
            {
              "number": 24,
              "title": "Server walking skeleton: kernel, core seam, /health, MCP auth, GHCR image",
              "state": "CLOSED",
              "stateReason": "COMPLETED",
              "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T17:56:01Z",
              "body": "## Parent\n\n#22\n\n## What to build\n\nStand up the James server as Node + TypeScript with ports and adapters, built like BlaakTasks. Copy the generic kernel from BlaakTasks with a provenance header (`LanguageModel`, `ModelMemo`, the Anthropic and Notion adapters, fakes); no fork. Introduce the application core as the one seam the HTTP API and MCP server sit on, with in-memory fakes for the secondary ports and a `StateStore` over the data directory.\n\nThe server exposes an unauthenticated `/health` and an MCP endpoint guarded by `MCP_TOKEN`. Merging to main builds `ghcr.io/jblaak/james` (`node:24-slim` with Chromium).\n\nThis is the prefactor every other ticket builds on. See #2 and #18.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Kernel files copied from BlaakTasks carry a provenance header; core tests run against the fakes with `pnpm test`\n- [ ] `GET /health` returns OK without auth\n- [ ] MCP requests without a valid `MCP_TOKEN` (≥ 32 chars, timing-safe compare) get 401; 10 failures in 15 minutes return 429\n- [ ] Request bodies are capped\n- [ ] A GitHub Actions workflow builds and pushes `ghcr.io/jblaak/james` on merge to main\n\n## Blocked by\n\nNone (can start immediately)\n",
              "repository": {
                "nameWithOwner": "JBlaak/James"
              },
              "blockedBy": {
                "nodes": []
              },
              "closedByPullRequestsReferences": {
                "nodes": [
